<title>AUSTRALIA: RTRS-Australian GDP rises 0.8 pct in Sept qtr.</title>
<headline>RTRS-Australian GDP rises 0.8 pct in Sept qtr.</headline>
<dateline>CANBERRA 1996-12-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Australia's seasonally adjusted average gross domestic product (GDP-A), which averages income, expenditure and production measures of GDP, rose 0.8 percent in the September quarter, the Australian Bureau of Statistics said.</p>
<p>Real GDP-A in the year to September rose 3.8 percent.</p>
<p>June quarter GDP-A remained unrevised at a rise of 0.1 percent.</p>
<p>Most economists had forecast September quarter GDP-A to rise 0.9 percent, for a year-on-year increase of around 3.4 percent.  </p>
<p>The bureau detailed quarterly GDP data as follows in pct-</p>
<p>				   Sept 96   June 96  Sept 95   Yr/Yr</p>
<p>	   GDP measures</p>
<p>    GDP-income based	   +1.2     +0.1    +1.8	+3.8</p>
<p>    GDP-expenditure based    +0.7     +0.1    +1.6	+3.9</p>
<p>    GDP-production based     +0.5     +0.1    +1.6	+3.6</p>
<p>    GDP-average		  +0.8     +0.1    +1.7	+3.8</p>
<p>				   Sept 96   June 96  Sept 95   Yr/Yr</p>
<p>Final Consumption Expenditure -</p>
<p>    Private			 +0.3     +0.6    +1.1	+3.3</p>
<p>    Government		    -1.2     -1.1    -3.1	+2.0</p>
<p>Gross Fixed Capital Expenditure -</p>
<p>Private</p>
<p>    Dwellings			-2.7    +2.5    -1.0     -12.3</p>
<p>    Non-dwelling construction +10.6    +2.4   +16.0	+9.8</p>
<p>    Equipment			+6.9    -0.1    -3.4     +22.4</p>
<p>    Real Estate transfers	-8.5    +8.0    -5.3	+2.5</p>
<p>    Total private		  +3.9    +1.6    +0.4	+8.4</p>
<p>				   Sept 96   June 96  Sept 95   Yr/Yr</p>
<p>Gross Fixed Capital Expenditure (FCE) (cont)-</p>
<p>    Total public		 -11.9     -5.4    +3.3     -12.5</p>
<p>Gross Total FCE		   +0.6     +0.1    +1.0	+3.8</p>
<p>    Domestic Final Demand     +0.1     +0.2    +0.3	+3.2</p>
<p>    Gross Nat'l Expenditure   +0.3     +0.8    +0.4	+3.6</p>
<p>    Exports of goods/services +1.2     -1.3    +4.7	+9.5</p>
<p>    Imports of goods/services -0.6     +1.9    -0.8	+8.3</p>
<p>(All figures at average 1989/90 prices)</p>
<p>-- Sydney newsroom 61-2 9373-1800</p>
